\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Unless a section says otherwise, ${\mathbb{N}}=\{1,2,\ldots\}$,
${\mathbb{Z}}$ is the ring of integers, ${\mathbb{Q}},{\mathbb{R}},{\mathbb{C}}$ are the rational, real, and complex
fields, $[N]=\{1,\ldots,\lfloor N\rfloor\}$, and $\log$ is the natural
logarithm. For real functions of a parameter tending to infinity,
$f\ll g$ and $f=O(g)$ mean $|f|\leq Cg$ eventually for some fixed $C$;
$f\gg g$ reverses this comparison; $f=o(g)$ means $f/g\to0$;
$f\sim g$ means $f/g\to1$; and $f\asymp g$ means both order bounds.
A subscript on $O$ or $\ll$ specifies allowed constant dependence.
The expression $n^{o(1)}$ in an upper bound means growth smaller than
$n^\epsilon$ for each fixed $\epsilon>0$. Local definitions govern any
exception, finite-field convention, or use of the same symbol for another
object. An asymptotic claim is not automatically an effective algorithm.



Count reduced residue classes $a\pmod d$, meaning $1\leq a\leq d$ and $\gcd(a,d)=1$. There are $\phi(d)$ of them. The notation $\gg\phi(d)$ asks for a positive proportion with an absolute lower bound independent of $d$.

Euler\textquotesingle s totient is $\phi(n)=\#\{1\leq a\leq n:\gcd(a,n)=1\}$. Distinct totient values are counted once unless the question explicitly asks for their preimage multiplicity. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]} \href{https://www.erdosproblems.com/971}{[S2]}

\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Let $p(a,d)$ be the least prime congruent to $a\pmod{d}$. Does there exist a constant $c>0$ such that, for all large $d$, $ p(a,d) > (1+c)\phi(d)\log d $ for $\gg \phi(d)$ many values of $a$? \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\paragraph{Residual task reported by the archive.}

The embedded review (2026-08-17) records: Establish a positive proportion for every sufficiently large d. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
For every sufficiently large modulus, do a positive proportion of its reduced residue classes have their first prime exceed the proposed totient-logarithm threshold?

